<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=torpedo">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Weapons and war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Torpedo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1866">1866</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Whitehead torpedo, fish torpedo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A torpedo is a self-propelled underwater weapon with an explosive warhead that detonates on contact with or close to its target. The word first named mines and spar torpedoes, used by Cornelius Drebbel, Robert Fulton and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>. In 1864 the naval officer Giovanni Luppis gave the English engineer Robert Whitehead plans for a floating weapon at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rijeka">Fiume</a>, now Rijeka in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>; Whitehead made from it the first modern self-propelled torpedo, run underwater by compressed air, and presented it to the Austrian Imperial Naval commission on 21 December 1866. On 16 January 1878 the Turkish steamer Intibah became the first ship sunk by self-propelled torpedoes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/torpedo.jpg" alt="Torpedo" width="330" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bidgee · CC BY-SA 3.0 au · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mark_8_torpedo_in_Germanton_Park.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1866</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Fiume (Rijeka, Croatia)</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Robert Whitehead · Giovanni Luppis · <a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Around 1280 Hasan al-Rammah described a rocket-driven incendiary craft that moved across the water, often cited as an early torpedo, but historians class it as a surface-skimming rocket; until about 1900 the word also covered mines and spar torpedoes.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=torpedo">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
